import{_ as o,o as a,c as t,a2 as n}from"./chunks/framework.CHeM0PsO.js";const g=JSON.parse(`{"title":"ADR 0008 — Deep links resolved natively via WebView redirect, not @capacitor/app's JS listener","description":"","frontmatter":{"status":"Accepted","date":"2026-08-09T00:00:00.000Z","deciders":"kasunben","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/sovereignfs/sovereign-mobile/blob/main/docs/adrs/0008-deep-link-native-handoff.md"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"apps/mobile/adrs/0008-deep-link-native-handoff.md","filePath":"apps/mobile/adrs/0008-deep-link-native-handoff.md"}`),i={name:"apps/mobile/adrs/0008-deep-link-native-handoff.md"};function c(d,e,s,r,l,h){return a(),t("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="adr-0008-—-deep-links-resolved-natively-via-webview-redirect-not-capacitor-app-s-js-listener" tabindex="-1">ADR 0008 — Deep links resolved natively via WebView redirect, not <code>@capacitor/app</code>&#39;s JS listener <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0008-—-deep-links-resolved-natively-via-webview-redirect-not-capacitor-app-s-js-listener" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;ADR 0008 — Deep links resolved natively via WebView redirect, not `@capacitor/app`&#39;s JS listener&quot;">​</a></h1><h2 id="status" tabindex="-1">Status <a class="header-anchor" href="#status" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Status&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Accepted. Decided in this repo directly during epic task 20.14&#39;s implementation — like ADR 0006, this has no <code>sovereign-desktop</code> precedent that transfers as-is, though the <em>shape</em> of the fix (native code hands the raw URL to the local page as a query param; TypeScript resolves it) does mirror <code>sovereign-desktop</code>&#39;s own <code>navigate_to_deep_link</code> (see that repo&#39;s epic task 17.3).</p><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Context&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>@capacitor/app</code> ships a JS-facing <code>App.addListener(&#39;appUrlOpen&#39;, ...)</code> API and a <code>getLaunchUrl()</code> method, and its own docs describe this as the standard way to handle custom URL scheme links. It looked like the obvious choice at first — no native code needed beyond registering the scheme.</p><p>It doesn&#39;t work reliably here, because of how this shell&#39;s WebView already behaves once an instance is loaded. <code>MainViewController.swift</code>&#39;s <code>enterRemoteMode()</code> (added for the RFC 0083 device bridge, workstream 0003 leg 4) explicitly removes Capacitor&#39;s own injected scripts and its <code>&quot;bridge&quot;</code> <code>WKScriptMessageHandler</code> — the mechanism <code>@capacitor/app</code>&#39;s JS API depends on — whenever the WebView shows a remote instance, and only restores them when navigating back to the local bundled page (<code>enterLocalMode()</code>). <code>NavigationPolicyWebViewClient</code>&#39;s Android equivalent doesn&#39;t remove Capacitor&#39;s bridge the same way, but the underlying problem is the same on both platforms regardless: <code>onboarding.ts</code>&#39;s <code>loadInstance()</code> calls <code>window.location.assign(url)</code> — a real, full top-level navigation away from the local page&#39;s own origin (<code>capacitor://localhost</code> on iOS, <code>https://localhost</code> on Android) to the remote instance&#39;s <code>https://</code> origin, per <a href="./0006-history-based-instance-switch-affordance.html">ADR 0006</a>. That destroys the local page&#39;s JS execution context entirely, including any <code>App.addListener(&#39;appUrlOpen&#39;, ...)</code> callback registered from it. A deep link tapped while the app is already showing a loaded instance — the most common real-world case, not an edge case — would have no live JS listener left to deliver it to.</p><p><code>getLaunchUrl()</code> alone would only cover cold launch (where the local page&#39;s JS context is guaranteed live, since <code>boot()</code> hasn&#39;t yet called <code>location.assign()</code>), leaving the warm-launch case broken. Handling the two cases through two different mechanisms (JS <code>getLaunchUrl()</code> for cold, something else for warm) was rejected as more moving parts than a single consistent fix.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Decision&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Both platforms&#39; native entry points intercept the <code>sovereign://</code> URL directly — before any JS ever sees it — and force the WebView to navigate to the local origin with the raw URL attached as <code>?deeplink=&lt;url&gt;</code>, regardless of what the WebView currently shows:</p><ul><li><strong>iOS:</strong> <code>AppDelegate.swift</code>&#39;s <code>application(_:open:options:)</code> calls a new <code>MainViewController.loadDeepLink(_:)</code>, which <code>webView?.load()</code>s <code>capacitor://localhost/?deeplink=&lt;percent-encoded-url&gt;</code> directly. Kept on <code>MainViewController</code> (not <code>AppDelegate</code>) since that class already owns <code>webView</code> access and the local/remote mode bookkeeping — <code>decidePolicyFor</code> sees this as a same-origin-to-local navigation and calls <code>enterLocalMode()</code> through its existing logic, no special-casing needed there.</li><li><strong>Android:</strong> <code>MainActivity.java</code>&#39;s <code>load()</code> (cold launch, via <code>getIntent()</code>) and <code>onNewIntent()</code> (warm launch) both route through a new <code>handleDeepLinkIntent(Intent)</code>, which — when the intent is <code>ACTION_VIEW</code> with a <code>sovereign:</code> scheme — calls <code>bridge.getWebView().loadUrl(LOCAL_ORIGIN + &quot;?deeplink=&quot; + Uri.encode(...))</code>. This is the same pattern <code>dispatchKeyEvent</code>&#39;s back-button handling already established (loading <code>LOCAL_ORIGIN + &quot;?manage=1&quot;</code> directly rather than trusting WebView history state) — reusing a precedented, already-verified technique rather than inventing a second one.</li></ul><p>TypeScript-side resolution (<code>src/deep-link.ts</code>&#39;s <code>resolveDeepLink</code>, consumed from <code>main.ts</code>) is a near-verbatim port of <code>sovereign-desktop</code>&#39;s own <code>src/deep-link.ts</code> — same scheme, same host-matching contract, same <code>KnownDeepLink | UnknownDeepLink | null</code> shape — since that logic has nothing platform-specific in it and already has full test coverage to copy forward. The one deliberate deviation: a known deep link resolves via <code>window.location.assign()</code>, not <code>.replace()</code>, matching ADR 0006&#39;s own history-preservation rule (<code>onboarding.ts</code>&#39;s <code>loadInstance()</code>) rather than desktop&#39;s <code>.replace()</code> — desktop has no back-gesture affordance depending on history to protect.</p><h2 id="rejected-alternatives" tabindex="-1">Rejected alternatives <a class="header-anchor" href="#rejected-alternatives" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Rejected alternatives&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong><code>@capacitor/app</code>&#39;s <code>addListener(&#39;appUrlOpen&#39;, ...)</code> / <code>getLaunchUrl()</code> only.</strong> The obvious first choice; rejected once <code>enterRemoteMode()</code>&#39;s bridge teardown made clear it cannot reliably fire once an instance is loaded — see Context above. Would need a second, different mechanism for cold launch regardless, since <code>getLaunchUrl()</code> and <code>appUrlOpen</code> are different APIs with different lifecycles.</li><li><strong>Universal Links (iOS) / App Links (Android) instead of a custom <code>sovereign://</code> scheme.</strong> Requires a <code>apple-app-site-association</code> / <code>assetlinks.json</code> file hosted at a domain this project doesn&#39;t control the way a per-instance self-hosted deployment would need (each user&#39;s own instance domain, not a single canonical one) — the <code>sovereign://</code> custom scheme sovereign-desktop already established doesn&#39;t have this problem and this shell should match it for a consistent cross-platform link format users and instance operators can rely on.</li></ul><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Consequences&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>Any future feature that also wants to force-navigate the WebView from native code must go through the same <code>LOCAL_ORIGIN</code>/<code>capacitor://localhost</code> local-origin redirect pattern, not a new ad hoc mechanism — two platforms already have two slightly different flavors of this (<code>dispatchKeyEvent</code>&#39;s back handling, now this), and a third would be worth extracting into a shared helper rather than copying again.</li><li><code>main.ts</code>&#39;s <code>boot()</code> now checks <code>?deeplink=</code> before the stored-active- instance shortcut, so a deep link always wins over silently reopening whatever was last active — matches <code>sovereign-desktop</code>&#39;s own ordering.</li><li><code>MainViewController.swift</code> and <code>MainActivity.java</code> both gain a small amount of native code for something that, on a platform where <code>@capacitor/app</code>&#39;s listener actually worked end-to-end, would have needed none. Accepted as the cost of this shell&#39;s own architecture (<code>location.assign()</code>-based instance loading, bridge isolation at the local/remote boundary) rather than a generic Capacitor limitation.</li></ul>',15)])])}const m=o(i,[["render",c]]);export{g as __pageData,m as default};
